.TH TERMNAV 1 "October 2026" "TermNav 1.1.0"
.SH NAME
termnav \- keyboard-driven, three-pane terminal file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termnav
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIdirectory\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Browse parent, current and preview panes with vim keys or arrow keys.
Directories appear before files. Text previews are bounded to 64 KiB.
Copy and delete run in a background thread with live progress counters.
Without a directory argument, start in the Home dashboard. A directory argument
starts directly in the browser. Press w to switch between Home and browser.
F1 jumps to Home from the browser, preview, help or feature panels.
.SH HOME DASHBOARD
Quick-access folder cards, a file table, and live drive capacity bars.
Tab cycles folders, files and drives; arrows choose; Enter opens the selection.
Drive bars show space available to the current user out of total capacity,
with amber at 85% occupied and red at 95%. Capacities refresh every five seconds.
Windows volumes mounted in WSL are included. Narrow terminals hide sidebars.
Esc returns to the browser. File actions retain their usual confirmation prompts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --home
Start on Home even when a directory is supplied. Demo launchers use this option.
.TP
.B -a, --all
Show hidden files.
.TP
.B --ascii
Use ASCII file-type symbols.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable colors. Also honors NO_COLOR.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print usage.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Print version.
.SH KEYS
j/k or arrows move; h/l or arrows navigate; Enter enters a directory or previews a file;
Tab expands any preview in the browser; w opens Home;
gg/G jumps first/last; J/K scrolls preview; / filters; Space marks;
v marks visible entries; y copies paths; p pastes; c copies to a directory;
a creates a directory; r renames; d trashes after typing trash; X permanently deletes after typing delete;
u restores the latest trash batch; T opens the trash browser;
t opens operations; D explores disk usage; b/f navigate history; H picks history;
: opens the searchable command palette;
o goes to a path; ~ goes home; e edits with EDITOR; . toggles hidden;
s cycles sort; R refreshes; ? opens help; q quits.
.SH FULL-SCREEN PREVIEW
Enter on a file or Tab on any entry opens a read-only full-screen viewer.
j/k scroll; h/l pan horizontally; PageUp/PageDown page; gg/G jump to ends;
0 resets horizontal position; R refreshes; Esc, Tab or q closes the viewer.
Binary files show sixteen-byte hex rows with offsets and an ASCII column.
Text shows line numbers and lightweight per-line syntax coloring.
Preview content is bounded to the first 64 KiB; truncation is labelled.
File operation keys are inactive inside the viewer. Browser selection, marks,
and filter persist when it closes. Images use native Sixel rasters on terminals
reporting support, with a pane-sized 1280x960 maximum and adaptive image colors.
Other terminals use Unicode half blocks; ASCII mode uses plain blocks.
Image previews use optional Python Pillow. PDFs show text from their first five pages using Poppler.
ZIP/tar archives list their contents without extraction. Rich previews are
bounded by memory, CPU, output and timeout limits; missing helpers show errors.
.SH OPERATIONS
The dashboard lists the active job, up to 16 queued jobs, and 32 recent results.
Space pauses or resumes the active selection; x cancels active or queued work.
Copy progress includes byte counts, average speed and elapsed time.
.SH TRASH
Trash payloads live beside their original parent in private .termnav-trash-UID
directories; records persist under XDG_DATA_HOME/termnav/trash or
~/.local/share/termnav/trash. This is separate from desktop recycle bins.
Restore refuses to overwrite an existing name. Undo applies to trash only.
T opens recovery; Enter restores an individual item; u restores the latest batch.
Restore moved parent directories before their children. Empty bins may remain.
.SH DISK USAGE AND HISTORY
D starts a background scan including hidden files. Allocated blocks are shown
with bars and percentages, alongside apparent totals. Enter drills down; h goes
to the parent; o reveals the selection in the file browser. R rescans.
Symlinks are never followed; hard links count per entry.
b/f go back/forward, restoring filters and selection. H opens the history picker.
History retains 64 locations per session; a new branch replaces forward history.
.SH FILES
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/termnav/config or ~/.config/termnav/config.
Supported settings: show_hidden=true|false, ascii=true|false,
sort=name|size|modified.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
EDITOR names the editor executable (no arguments), default vi.
NO_COLOR disables colors. TERM must describe an interactive terminal.
TERMNAV_IMAGE=auto (default) probes Sixel; sixel forces raster output;
blocks selects the portable fallback. Do not force Sixel on unsupported terminals.
.SH NOTES
X deletion is permanent. Existing destination files are never overwritten.
Cancellation keeps completed work; directory copies may be partial.
Symlinks are copied as links and are never traversed by recursive copy/delete.
